  'Planning a Proposal on the Water: What to Settle First',
  'Planning a Proposal on the Water: What to Settle First',
  'A boat to yourselves or two seats at sunset, how to time the question, who to tell beforehand and what cannot come on board. The decisions, in order.',
  'proposal on the water',
  'You have the ring and you have decided on a boat. Here is what to settle before you book, so the day holds one surprise, and it is the one you planned.',
  'Boattime Yacht Charters',
  5,
  false,
  '/private-charter.jpeg',
  'Sun Goddess under way on the Gold Coast Broadwater on a clear day',
  'https://www.boattimeyachtcharters.com/boattime-news/planning-a-proposal-on-the-water-what-to-settle-first',
  '<p>You have the ring, and you have decided the question should be asked on the water. It is a good setting for it: the light does half the work, and there is nowhere else either of you needs to be. It also runs to a timetable you do not control, so the planning is mostly about a few decisions made in the right order.</p><p>There are two ways to do it. If you want the boat to yourselves, the page that takes the enquiry is <a href="/private-yacht-charter">private yacht charter on the Gold Coast</a>. If two seats at sunset is closer to what you have in mind, that is the <a href="/luxury-broadwater-cruise">Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise</a>. The rest of this guide helps you choose between them and plan around the one you pick.</p><h2>1. Decide whether you want an audience</h2><p>This is the first decision, and it is really a question about your partner rather than about boats.</p><ul><li><p><strong>A private charter</strong> means the vessel carries your group and nobody else. You choose the time, the route and who is on board, whether that is the two of you or both families waiting out of sight.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise</strong> is booked seat by seat on Sun Goddess, so you share the boat with other guests. It runs for two and a half hours on the Broadwater, leaving at five o''clock, and tickets include a nibbles share plate for two with a licensed bar on board.</p></li></ul><p>Some people would love a deck of strangers cheering. Others would rather the moment belonged to two people. If you are not sure which your partner is, plan for the quieter version.</p><h2>2. Work back from the light</h2><p>Most people picture the question being asked at sunset. That gives you a fixed point to plan from.</p><p>Look up the sunset time for your date, then work backwards. On the scheduled sunset cruise the departure time is set, so check how the two line up before you book. On a private charter the timing is yours to arrange, and it is worth asking for a departure that puts you on the water well before the light goes rather than racing it.</p><p>Ask earlier than feels necessary. If the ring comes out as the colour starts, you have the rest of the sunset to enjoy being engaged, and the photos are taken in good light instead of the last of it.</p><h2>3. Tell the right people beforehand</h2><p>A proposal is a surprise for one person. Everybody else who can help should know.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Tell the operator when you enquire.</strong> Say that it is a proposal, when you would like to ask, and whether you want it kept quiet. Ask what the crew can do to help on the day.</p></li><li><p><strong>Ask about a photographer before you book one.</strong> If you want the moment photographed, raise it with the operator first, so everyone knows who is coming aboard and where they can stand.</p></li><li><p><strong>Brief one accomplice.</strong> If family or friends are involved, one person should hold the plan and the timings, and it should not be you.</p></li></ul><h2>4. Check what can come on board</h2><p>The rule that catches people out: <strong>no outside food or drink is allowed on board.</strong> That includes the bottle of champagne you were saving for the moment.</p><p>It is easily solved, as long as you solve it early. Both vessels have licensed bars, and on a private charter the catering is arranged ahead through the operator''s culinary partners, from grazing boards and canapés to a private chef. If you want a particular bottle opened the moment the answer is yes, ask for it when you arrange the catering.</p><p>Flowers, a sign or anything else you plan to bring should be raised at the same time. Ask rather than assume.</p><h2>5. Plan for the weather you get</h2><p>You cannot book the sky. Both vessels have covered decks and indoor spaces, so a grey evening changes the backdrop without cancelling the plan. Decide in advance where on board you would ask if the open deck is not an option, so you are not improvising with a ring box in your pocket.</p><p>Wind matters more than cloud on the day. Hair, hats and anything loose behave differently on an open deck, and a ring box is safer in a zipped pocket than in a jacket draped over a chair.</p><h2>6. Get the arrival right</h2><p>Boarding is at Marina Mirage Seaworld Drive, Main Beach, about ten minutes from Surfers Paradise and fifteen from Broadbeach. The boat leaves on schedule, so build in more time than you think you need. Arriving flustered is a poor start to the evening you have in mind.</p><p>If your partner does not know where you are going, think about how much the marina gives away, and have a line ready. A sunset cruise for no particular reason is a perfectly believable plan.</p><p>Think about afterwards as well. Once you are back at the marina there will be phone calls to make, so book the dinner or the drive home before the day, not on the dock.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can we have the boat to ourselves for a proposal?</h3><p>Yes. A private charter carries your group and nobody else, and the enquiry starts on the private yacht charter page. Sun Goddess takes up to 135 guests on a day charter and Mermaid Spirit up to 150, so a charter also suits a proposal followed by a party with family and friends.</p><h3>Can we propose on the sunset cruise instead?</h3><p>Yes. The Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise is booked seat by seat, so other guests will be on board. It suits a couple who are happy to share the moment, or who want a quiet corner of the deck and the skyline behind them.</p><h3>Can we bring our own champagne?</h3><p>No outside food or drink is permitted on board. Both vessels have licensed bars, and on a private charter you can ask for something particular when the catering is arranged.</p><h3>What happens if it rains?</h3><p>Both vessels have covered decks and indoor spaces, so the evening goes ahead under cover. Choose a second spot on board in advance, in case the open deck is wet or windy.</p><h3>Will the crew help with the surprise?</h3><p>Tell the operator it is a proposal when you enquire, and ask what the crew can do on the day. The earlier they know, the easier it is to plan around.</p><h2>Start with the date</h2><p>A private charter takes one group at a time, so the date comes first. Send the date, the time of day and the fact that it is a proposal through the <a href="/private-yacht-charter">private yacht charter</a> page. If two seats at sunset suit you better, pick your sailing on the <a href="/luxury-broadwater-cruise">Luxury Scenic Sunset Cruise</a> page.</p>',
